<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ranoji Scindia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1745">1745</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ranoji Shinde · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scindias">Scindias</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ranoji Scindia was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> commander and the founder of the Scindia dynasty of Gwalior. Rising in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a>, he received a share of the revenues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> around 1731 and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>. His sons, above all Mahadaji Scindia, made the family one of the strongest Maratha houses, and later rulers moved their capital to Gwalior.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranoji-scindia.jpg" alt="Ranoji Scindia" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amit20081980 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ranoji_Rao_Scindia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ranoji Scindia, also known as Ranoji Sendrak, was born about 1700 into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> family. His father, Jankoji Rao I, was hereditary patil, or village head, of Kanherkhed, a village in the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. In earlier centuries his forefathers had served as shiledars, cavalrymen, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, and had held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagirs</a>. As a young man he entered the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaji-vishwanath">Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath</a>. Ramchandrababa Sukhtankar, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>'s diplomats, recognised his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and had him made personal bodyguard to the Peshwa's son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Baji Rao was appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> at the age of twenty by Chhatrapati Shahu, the appointment aroused the jealousy of senior officials at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> court. He therefore promoted talented young men, barely out of their teens, to command his troops, among them Ranoji Scindia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malhar-rao-holkar">Malhar Rao Holkar</a>, the Pawar brothers, Pilaji Jadhav and Fateh Singh Bhosle. None of them came from families that had held hereditary deshmukhi rights under earlier rulers such as the Deccan Sultanates.</p><p>Ranoji was in charge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> campaign that Baji Rao began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in 1726, and after it he established his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> in 1731. He appointed Ramchandrababa Sukhtankar as his dewan, or administrator, and Yashaji Rambhaji as commander of his army, and himself spent most of his life on campaign. Some historians credit him with bringing the Kumbh Mela to Ujjain in 1732.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ranoji died on 3 July 1745. He had five sons: Jayappa Rao, Jyotiba Rao, Dattaji Rao, Tukoji Rao and Mahadji. The four eldest died in battles in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between 1750 and 1761. The dynasty went on to dominate central and northern India in the later 18th century through its presence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, its collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauth">chauth</a> from neighbouring rulers and a modernised army, and his descendants ruled the princely state of Gwalior under the British.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ranoji Scindia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ranoji Scindia</b>, a commander under Peshwa Baji Rao I, received a share of Malwa around 1731, ruling from Ujjain.</blockquote><a href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kanherkhed, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Shujalpur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.65,74.23&amp;year=1735" aria-label="Kanherkhed on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6565 1509 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ranoji Scindia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7015 1779h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7015 1779h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.65,74.23&amp;year=1735">What was here in 1735? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
